[23:01] Both agents passed every practice customer in their last run: Brief A 10 of 10, Brief B 3 of 3.
[23:01] 
[23:01] **How they got there**
[23:01] - **Brief A:** 8/10 on the first run, then 9/10, then 10/10.
[23:01] - **Brief B:** 2/3 on the first run, then 3/3.
[23:01] 
[23:01] Each fix round changed `out/gen_agents.py`, then lowered, checked (0 errors, 0 warnings each time), saved and tested again. That was two fix rounds for A and one for B, within the limit of three.
[23:01] 
[23:01] **What failed and what I changed**
[23:01] - **PUB-07 (holiday):** the model treated the holiday on 26 Oct as "after the deadline". Code now builds a calendar where every day says what to do for each product, such as "offer the next business day Tue 27 Oct".
[23:01] - **PUB-06 (floor offer):** the model left out "the rest stays overdue" and paraphrased the debt statement, dropping "collections" and the days late. The debt statement is now a fixed spoken line built in code, and the floor offer has required wording.
[23:01] - **PUB-12 (B):** the model made two offers on an early-band product, where the brief allows one. Its read-back also said "I have recorded" before the promise existed. I tightened the persuasion rules and made the read-back say "I will record ...".
[23:01] - **PUB-10 (tool failure):** the agent retried without telling the customer the first attempt failed. The judge also counted "this call is recorded" in the opening as saying "recorded" too early. The retry step now opens with a fixed sentence saying nothing is saved yet. The opening now says "we keep a recording of this call", which still covers rule 1.
[23:01] 
[23:01] **Design**
[23:01] - Moves between steps and the close values depend on tool results where the brief allows it: identity passed, three wrong answers, promise recorded, second promise failure, transfer done, callback booked.
[23:01] - Every ending is declared in `out/<slot>.closing.json`, and `tools/lower.py` writes the `close_call` steps.
[23:01] - Some endings still depend on the model calling the right ending: refused, dispute, paid, hardship, declined, unclear. In Brief B the final ending also depends on the model, because it follows the promises and notes the model made.
[23:01] 
[23:01] **Limits**
[23:01] - Each customer ran once per round. The README warns that one green run is a hint, not proof.
[23:01] - Brief B has only 3 practice customers, so restructuring after a recorded promise and a double `create_promise` failure in B were never tested.
[23:01] - I did not run the `--style` variants or `--reps 3`.
[23:01] 
